# Sparky Stereo OS's own packages win over Debian's (the stereo kernel, NVIDIA's open modules
# with our patches, KWin and KScreen with stereo 3D, the edition's packages), so an update
# never swaps the stereo stack for stock packages; we rebuild them when Debian moves.
# Installed by the sparky-stereo-apt package as /etc/apt/preferences.d/sparky-stereo (its one
# owner on an installed system); the development VM copies this same file there.
Package: *
Pin: release o=Sparky Stereo OS
Pin-Priority: 1001

# Bino comes only from Sparky Stereo OS (Daniel, 2026-10-06): our build decodes any input
# but always outputs full side by side, the edition's contract with KWin. Debian's and
# deb-multimedia's Bino have their own output modes (anaglyph, interleaved, half side by
# side...), which would hand KWin a picture outside the contract, so they never install, on any
# architecture: apt_preferences(5) matches a plain name on the native architecture only, ":any" on all
# of them (ORDER 318).
Package: bino:any
Pin: release o=Debian
Pin-Priority: -1

Package: bino:any
Pin: release o=Unofficial Multimedia Packages
Pin-Priority: -1

# deb-multimedia's keyring: the repository carries deb-multimedia's own package (its 2024.9.1, only repackaged through
# Lean Code as 2024.9.1+lc1), so that sparky-stereo-apt can depend on it before deb-multimedia is in the sources. Once
# it is, deb-multimedia's own package, which follows its key, is preferred: this one is only the way in.
# sparky-stereo-only-ours does not block deb-multimedia's (NOT_OURS in apt/make-only-ours.py, ORDER 321).
Package: deb-multimedia-keyring
Pin: release o=Sparky Stereo OS
Pin-Priority: 400

# NVIDIA's 580 user space, which the edition carries from NVIDIA's own Debian 12 packages for Maxwell, Pascal and Volta
# (ORDER 354), is a candidate only where one of the two driver locks raises it: sparky-stereo-nvidia-legacy-580xx-driver-lock
# gives it 1005 in 09sparky-stereo-nvidia-legacy-580xx-driver-lock, and the main lock gives it 100; both files sort before
# this one, and apt_preferences(5) says "The first specific-form record matching an available package version determines
# the priority of the package version", so a lock decides first and this record only applies with no lock. Without it the
# general record above (1001) made these binaries the candidates on every system, and apt 3.3's solver, in a
# dist-upgrade, treats a package as obsolete when another binary of its source package has a higher source version at an
# equal or higher priority (apt-pkg/depsolver.cc, ObsoletedByNewerSourceVersion): Debian's nvidia-detect 550 (source
# nvidia-graphics-drivers) was replaced by our nvidia-kernel-support 580, which Provides, Conflicts and Replaces it, and
# nvidia-modprobe 580 came with it, on a machine whose hardware setup had chosen no NVIDIA stack (measured, r1103a). The
# set is the one the locks name (the source packages NVIDIA's own nvidia-driver-pinning package pins, and cuda-drivers*),
# only from this repository: NVIDIA's CUDA repository and Debian are not matched.
Package: src:nvidia-graphics-drivers:any src:nvidia-open:any cuda-drivers* src:nvidia-modprobe src:nvidia-persistenced src:nvidia-settings src:nvidia-xconfig src:libnvidia-nscq src:libnvsdm src:nvidia-fabricmanager src:nvidia-imex
Pin: release o=Sparky Stereo OS
Pin-Priority: -1
